WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
we're going to begin this hour with a  revealing first-hand account of what  happened inside the white house while  former president donald trump was in  office all of it according to former  secretary of defense mark esper he's got  a new book it's called a sacred oath  memoirs of a secretary of defense during  extraordinary times and it's a detailed  account of the nearly 18 months he  served under president trump from june  2019 to november of 2020. the former  secretary writes about the challenges of  working for trump including allegations  that the president suggested shooting at  protesters in the nation's capital  secretary esper joins us for his first  live television interview about this  book mr secretary thank you for being  here thanks great to be here uh i'm glad  you wrote the book i'm glad you served  in your role but i have to start with  kind of a process question which is why  did you accept this job in the first  place having just seen how president  trump treated your predecessor general  mattis you know i served my country  since the age of 18 when i went to west  point  10 years in the army active duty combat  gulf war another 11 years in the garden  reserve it was another chance for me to  once again serve my country in a way  that i thought could be meaningful and  help deliver on our nation's security  clearly it was meaningful as you write  in the book you had to stop a lot of bad  things from happening i i'd love to know  if there was a particular moment a high  point or really a low point where you  felt like the world order as we know it  or the country as we know it was in  danger of spinning out of control well  as i talk about my book it's june 1st  right we go from june 1st to june 2nd  protests around the country hundreds of  cities unrest the president is pushing  to call on active duty troops it is not  relenting and as we roll into june 3rd i  decide the night of june 2nd that i got  to get out there and say something the  republic feels wobbly  somebody needs to put you know their  hand on the wheel foot on the brake and  kind of calm things down and i thought  that me coming out publicly and saying  look i do not support invocation the  insurrection act it doesn't make sense  at this point in time that that might  help and i and i believe it did frankly  do you think that president trump fully  understood the consequences of his  actions when he was in office  with regard to this no he was just so  upset and angry about the protest in the  street that he wanted to suppress it he  wanted to get it under control you also  wrote  that president trump asked the military  if he could shoot missiles into mexico  um and you basically explained to him  that that's illegal it's an act of war  right and he said well we'll shoot those  missiles at cartels and and drug houses  um when you told him that what was his  response well look yeah he wanted to  shoot at drug labs and as i say i get it  right we we all are troubled by drugs in  america he was trying to find a way to  go after this but there are right ways  to do it and wrong ways to do it and  when i said no you can't it would be an  act of war illegal he said well look  nobody will know we they just won't find  out wow  in your book that um you wrote here you  said that our nation's institutions and  our democracy are only as strong as the  people who are serving in those roles  it's likely  that former president trump will run  again in 2024.  what are the consequences of him running  and possibly being nominated  as the next president of the united  states i'm glad you read that and  highlighted that and you're right i  think our institutions are only as  strong as the people in them i was  blessed to serve with wonderful people  to pentagon who really put country first  and so that's one of the things you have  to look at when you elect the president  who who will they bring in around them  who will be in their cabinet and i think  this is in some ways a cautionary tale  about what the future may hold if  president trump runs in 2024. what is at  stake i mean here we are talking about  ukraine um we're still always you know  keeping a close eye on china iran but  what is at stake  if president trump were to run again  well my view is whoever runs has to put  country first over self you have to have  integrity principles you have to be  willing to reach across the aisle and  advance the country's greater good and  president trump doesn't make those marks  for me so we have to be very cautious  about who he elect to be president is he  competent enough to hold office  he's held office but i would say for all  the republicans like myself who were  attracted to the policies he advanced  less regulation lower taxes a stronger  military and so on there's a whole other  crew of republicans out there who  advance those same principles without  the turmoil without the bitterness and  divisiveness and the courses that donald  trump brings like who well i'm not going  to start naming names today we'll see  the the 2024 will start after the  midterms right let's see who merges  let's see who who will who will find  some distance between them and donald  trump yeah  what does it say about the state of  politics in this country that after your  experience with president trump in the  2020 election you could not bring  yourself to  vote for president biden  well it's uh look i i work with  president biden if you will when i was  served on a senate foreign relations  committee i know who he is what he was i  i think we should have better choices  out there than what we have today out of  a nation of 330 million people we've got  to have a better set of candidates to  really help lead our country and they  have to put country first they have to  aspire to greater things do you have any  interest  i couldn't win an election i'm i'm too  much in the middle i guess i i think you  know i consider myself a reagan  republican in the book you talk about  how president trump is unprincipled uh  hearing you say that you think he's also  unfit i think is a new line that you've  crossed here and i i'm curious  you know you're going to get involved  now with the publication of this book  with a kind of tit-for-tat back and  forth with the former president so for  example he'd no commented on the  shooting missiles into mexico uh but he  says it's a lie that he suggested  shooting protesters in washington in  that fateful summer what's your response  to that people will choose to believe  who they want to believe i will tell you  that i sent this my my draft out to over  two dozen  four-star officers senior civilians even  a few cabinet members to review nearly  all or parts of the book and to get it  it helped me get it as accurate complete  and fair as possible so one of one of  the four no's that you and mark milley  had when you were trying to hold order  there in the administration was you  didn't want to politicize the department  of defense i remember speaking to  general mattis about his book when it  came out he was also not wanting to do  that your book seems to go further now  in this interview you're going further  are you worried with the book and with  your statements that you risk  politicizing the very department you  wanted to protect well look i'm clearly  i'm out of office now i was a political  appointee so there is some expectation  that you have one foot in the political  camp and one foot outside the political  camp but i'll tell you what the  department of defense has a strong ethos  it's a it's a resilient organization  we're blessed to have the the service  members  officer nco enlisted members serving  that we do i think the the institution  will endure it's for 240 some years it's  been defending america  it will endure quickly uh what is your  thoughts on the biden administration's  approach on the war in ukraine well as i  said i think it was mixed at first i  thought they were moving too slowly and  not enough i i said from day one we  should implement full sanctions and give  the ukrainians everything they need to  do the job but i think it's picked up i  think the president has done a good job  in terms of bringing along the allies i  think very good job in terms of the  strategic release of information to  reveal what putin was trying to do  surreptitiously so i think we're in a  much better spot today than where we  were initially all right secretary esper  as you put in the very front of the book  the oath of the secretary of defense is  to the constitution most important thing  thank you very much thank you all very  much thank you the book a sacred oath  goes on sale tomorrow

